# B87 — the other side of the disambiguation. `panic!` is a real compiler
# MACRO, not a call: it lowers to `ruxen_panic(msg)` plus an unreachable
# terminator. The B87 fix narrowed macro parsing to the closed set in
# `parser::macros::BUILTIN_MACRO_NAMES`, so this pins that `panic!` is still
# inside that set and still aborts.
#
# `after` must never reach stdout — that is what separates a macro that
# terminates from one that was quietly dropped.
def main
  puts "before"
  panic!("boom")
  puts "after"
end
